A||N||5||Monkeyman||Torque--Please help ASAP||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||08-21-2000||08:26 PM||||I have 3 corners of my new brakes done (including new sway bar end links and saddle bushings).  All I need to do is finish the left rear, bleed and install the new MC, bleed the brakes, and tighten the spindle/axle nuts.  Here's where my problem lies.<P>What torque is right for the nuts?????  I have to finish this up and be running by Thursday morning as I have to take the car out of town on business.  Help!!!<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000001||wilburt||08-21-2000||11:10 PM||||I only have the Haynes manual handy, but on page 188 it says the torque for the 'Hub nut' is:<P>new.......200 ft.lbs.<BR>used.......70 ft.lbs.<P>70 ft.lbs. used? What is that? I have read many times that you should not re-use the hub nut. Hope this is the nut you're refering to.||||reg||4
Z||000002||Oreif||08-22-2000||02:23 AM||||That is the wrong section in the Haynes manual, Look on page 223 section 11.<P>Per the Pontiac Service Manual, You are to initially tighten them to 74 ftlbs, Then lower car with steel wheels on it and tighten to 200 ftlbs. The Service manual and every other Fiero service manual does not say how to do this with aluminum wheels. They do mention that the procedure is for steel wheels but nada for aluminum.<BR> I have a set of steel wheels I use for storing the car over winter and I did exactly as the manual said. All manuals say to discard old nut.  NEVER use old nuts. These are a "clinch" type of nut. Once removed they are no good.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||06:20 AM||||OK.  200ft.lbs. it is.  Seems like alot, though.  I thought I remembered 90ft. lbs. so I tried that on the passenger side and couldn't turn the bare hub (no wheel) by hand.  What happens if you overtighten it by mistake, then back it off?  Will everything still be kosher?  Or would I need to buy new bearings, etc?  So far everything's gone as well as can be expected although I'm gonna have some rusty calipers 'cause I got brake fluid on them trying to put the line on.<P>Thanx, guys.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ur2biteme||08-24-2000||10:59 PM||||i just pulled the axle recently to check the inner c.v. joint.  i have the turbo wheels, and all you need to do to torque the hub nut with the wheel on is to remember to knock out the center insert on these wheels before putting them back on.  i'm not sure what the g.t. wheels are like though.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||08-25-2000||06:18 AM||||Thanx, but it's already done.  I have aftermarket wheels with a center cap that covers the lug nuts.  I could torque them down before I reinstalled the center cap.||||reg||1
